**Why bypass the persistence context:**
- Bulk import of millions of rows — every loaded entity is tracked, every change dirty-checked.
- Persistence context grows unbounded -> multi-GB heap -> OOM.
- Snapshot copies double memory consumption.

**What `StatelessSession` skips:**
- **No first-level cache** — no entity tracking.
- **No dirty checking** — explicit `update()` calls only.
- **No lazy loading proxies** — eager or manual fetch.
- **No lifecycle callbacks** — `@PrePersist`, `@PostLoad` don't fire.
- **No cascades** — children must be persisted explicitly.

**Obtaining a StatelessSession:**

```java
@PersistenceContext EntityManager em;

public void bulkImport(List<Product> products) {
    SessionFactory sf = em.unwrap(Session.class).getSessionFactory();
    try (StatelessSession s = sf.openStatelessSession()) {
        Transaction tx = s.beginTransaction();
        products.forEach(s::insert);
        tx.commit();
    }
}
```

**Lighter alternative — `clear()` on the regular session:**
- For chunked batch processing where you still want JPA features.
- Call `em.flush(); em.clear()` after every chunk (e.g., 50 records).
- Spring Batch chunk-oriented processing uses this pattern.

**Idiomatic unwrap:**

```java
@Service
public class UserLookupService {
    @PersistenceContext EntityManager em;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Optional<User> findByEmail(String email) {
        Session session = em.unwrap(Session.class);
        return Optional.ofNullable(
            session.byNaturalId(User.class).using("email", email).load()
        );
    }
}
```

**`StatelessSession` for 10M-row import:**

```java
@Service
public class BulkImportService {
    @PersistenceContext EntityManager em;

    public void importFromCsv(Path csvFile) {
        Session session = em.unwrap(Session.class);
        SessionFactory sf = session.getSessionFactory();
        try (StatelessSession stateless = sf.openStatelessSession();
             Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(csvFile)) {
            Transaction tx = stateless.beginTransaction();
            lines.forEach(line -> stateless.insert(parseLine(line)));
            tx.commit();
        }
    }
}
```

**`ScrollableResults` for streaming:**

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public void processAllOrders() {
    Session session = em.unwrap(Session.class);
    try (ScrollableResults<Order> results = session
            .createQuery("FROM Order o ORDER BY o.id", Order.class)
            .setFetchSize(100)
            .scroll(ScrollMode.FORWARD_ONLY)) {
        int count = 0;
        while (results.next()) {
            processOrder(results.get());
            if (++count % 100 == 0) { em.flush(); em.clear(); }
        }
    }
}
```

**Dynamic filter via `enableFilter()`:**

```java
@Entity
@FilterDef(name = "tenantFilter", parameters = @ParamDef(name = "tenantId", type = Long.class))
@Filter(name = "tenantFilter", condition = "tenant_id = :tenantId")
public class Order { /* ... */ }

@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<Order> findAllForCurrentTenant() {
    Session s = em.unwrap(Session.class);
    s.enableFilter("tenantFilter").setParameter("tenantId", CurrentTenant.get());
    return em.createQuery("FROM Order", Order.class).getResultList();
}
```

**Scenario:** save a new `Order` with 5 items, modify 2, delete 1.

```java
@Transactional
public void demo() {
    Order o = new Order();
    o.setTotalAmount(BigDecimal.ZERO);
    em.persist(o);

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        em.persist(new OrderItem(o, "Product " + i));
    }

    Order existing = em.find(Order.class, 42L);
    existing.setStatus(SHIPPED);
    existing.getItems().get(0).setQuantity(99);
    em.remove(existing.getItems().get(4));
}
// At commit, ActionQueue plays out:
//   INSERT INTO orders ...
//   INSERT INTO order_items ... x 5  (JDBC batched)
//   UPDATE orders SET status='SHIPPED' WHERE id=42 AND version=?
//   UPDATE order_items SET quantity=99 WHERE id=? AND version=?
//   DELETE FROM order_items WHERE id=? AND version=?
```

**Enable JDBC batching:**

```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        jdbc.batch_size: 50
        order_inserts: true
        order_updates: true
        batch_versioned_data: true
```

**`@DynamicUpdate` on wide tables:**

```java
@Entity
@DynamicUpdate
public class User {
    @Id Long id;
    String firstName, lastName, email, phone, /* ... 30 fields ... */ profileJson;
}

User u = em.find(User.class, 1L);
u.setPhone("new");
// Without @DynamicUpdate: UPDATE users SET first_name=?, last_name=?, ... phone=?, ... WHERE id=?
// With @DynamicUpdate:    UPDATE users SET phone=? WHERE id=? AND version=?
```

**Explicit flush before native query:**

```java
Order o = em.find(Order.class, 1L);
o.setStatus(SHIPPED);
em.flush();
List<Object[]> stats = em.createNativeQuery(
    "SELECT status, COUNT(*) FROM orders GROUP BY status").getResultList();
```

**Step 1 — Always-LAZY entity mapping:**

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
    @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id")
    Customer customer;

    @OneToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, mappedBy = "order")
    OrderInvoice invoice;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @BatchSize(size = 50)
    List<OrderItem> items;
}
```

**Step 2 — Opt in per query:**

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"customer", "items"})
    Optional<Order> findById(Long id);

    @Query("""
        select new com.acme.OrderSummary(o.id, c.name, o.totalAmount, o.status)
        from Order o join o.customer c
        where o.status = :status
    """)
    Page<OrderSummary> findSummaries(@Param("status") OrderStatus status, Pageable page);

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"customer"})
    Page<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status, Pageable page);
}
```

**Step 3 — Disable OSIV and return DTOs:**

```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    open-in-view: false
```

```java
@RestController
public class OrderController {
    @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
    public OrderDto getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        Order o = orderService.findById(id);
        return OrderDto.from(o);
    }
}
```

**Why this works:**
- No surprise EAGER fetches — every association load is explicit.
- No N+1 in dashboards — DTO projections flatten joins.
- No `LazyInitializationException` — DTOs are detached from the persistence context.
- `@BatchSize` cushions remaining lazy loads.

**Before (broken):**

```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id Long id;
    String name;
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    List<Order> orders;
}

@Service
public class UserService {
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public User findById(Long id) { return userRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow(); }
}

@RestController
public class UserController {
    @GetMapping("/users/{id}")
    public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return userService.findById(id);
    }
}
```

With `open-in-view = false`, this throws `LazyInitializationException` during JSON rendering.

**Fix 1 — DTO projection (gold standard):**

```java
public record UserDto(Long id, String name, List<OrderDto> orders) {
    public static UserDto from(User u) {
        return new UserDto(u.getId(), u.getName(),
            u.getOrders().stream().map(OrderDto::from).toList());
    }
}
public record OrderDto(Long id, OrderStatus status, BigDecimal total) {
    public static OrderDto from(Order o) {
        return new OrderDto(o.getId(), o.getStatus(), o.getTotal());
    }
}

@Service
public class UserService {
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public UserDto findById(Long id) {
        User u = userRepo.findWithOrdersById(id).orElseThrow();
        return UserDto.from(u);
    }
}

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"orders"})
    Optional<User> findWithOrdersById(Long id);
}
```

**Fix 2 — `@EntityGraph` (when returning entities):**

```java
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"orders", "orders.items"})
Optional<User> findDeepById(Long id);
```

**Fix 3 — `Hibernate.initialize()`:**

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public User findWithInitializedOrders(Long id) {
    User u = userRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    Hibernate.initialize(u.getOrders());
    return u;
}
```
